# Screen Creation Guidelines

## Overview
This document provides comprehensive guidelines for creating and organizing screens in our Expo Router application, including navigation patterns, naming conventions, and implementation details.

## Directory Structure Requirements

### Root Level Organization
- `_layout.tsx`: Root layout configuration for modals and global navigation
- Direct route files (e.g., `about.tsx`): For standalone pages
- Modal screens (e.g., `account.tsx`): For modal overlays

### Tab Navigation Structure
- Use `(tabs)` directory for tab-based navigation
- Tab configuration in `(tabs)/_layout.tsx`
- Home/default tab MUST use `(index)` directory name
- Other tabs should use normal folder names without parentheses (e.g., `profile`, `settings`)
- Must include `unstable_settings` in layout files
- NEVER place an `index.tsx` file at the same level as the `(tabs)` directory, as it will override the default tab's index page

### ❌ Incorrect Tab Structure
```
app/
├── (tabs)/
│   └── (index)/
│       └── index.tsx
├── index.tsx    # ❌ This will override the default tab's index page
└── _layout.tsx
```

### ✅ Correct Tab Structure
```
app/
├── (tabs)/
│   └── (index)/
│       └── index.tsx    # ✅ Default tab's index page
└── _layout.tsx
```

### Stack Navigation Structure
- Each stack must have its own directory
- Must include `_layout.tsx` for stack configuration
- Use `index.tsx` for the main screen
- Additional screens as direct files (e.g., `detail.tsx`)

## Naming Conventions

### Directory Names
- Tab groups: `(tabs)`
- Home/default tab: MUST use `(index)` (not `home` or `main`)
- Feature tabs: Use normal folder names without parentheses (e.g., `profile`, `settings`)
- Use parentheses `()` only for route groups and the home tab

### File Names
- Layouts: `_layout.tsx`
- Main screens: `index.tsx`
- Detail/sub screens: descriptive names (e.g., `detail.tsx`)
- Modal screens: can be placed in any directory with Stack navigation (e.g., `account.tsx` in root or `filter.tsx` in a tab directory)

## Implementation Guidelines

### 1. Adding New Tab
Create tab directory in `app/(tabs)/`:
```typescript
// File structure
app/
└── (tabs)/
    ├── (index)/            # Home/default tab
    │   ├── _layout.tsx     # Stack navigation config
    │   └── index.tsx       # Main screen
    └── profile/            # Feature tab (no parentheses)
        ├── _layout.tsx     # Stack navigation config
        └── index.tsx       # Main screen

// (tabs)/_layout.tsx
<Tabs.Screen 
  name="(index)" 
  options={{
    title: "Home",
  }}
/>
<Tabs.Screen 
  name="profile" 
  options={{
    title: "Profile",
  }}
/>

// profile/_layout.tsx
export const unstable_settings = {
  initialRouteName: "index",
}

export default function ProfileLayout() {
  return (
    <Stack>
      <Stack.Screen name="index" options={{ title: "Profile" }} />
    </Stack>
  )
}
```

### 2. Adding Sub-Pages
Add new screen file in tab directory:
```typescript
// File structure
app/
└── (tabs)/
    └── (index)/
        ├── _layout.tsx
        ├── index.tsx
        └── detail.tsx     # New sub-page

// _layout.tsx
<Stack>
  <Stack.Screen name="index" options={{ title: "Home" }} />
  <Stack.Screen name="detail" options={{ title: "Details" }} />
</Stack>

// Navigation usage
import { Link } from "expo-router"

// Relative path (same tab)
<Link href="detail">View Details</Link>

// Absolute path (from other tabs)
<Link href="/(index)/detail">View Details</Link>
```

### 3. Adding Modal Screens
Modal screens can be added at any level where there's a Stack navigator. This includes:
- Root level for app-wide modals
- Tab level for tab-specific modals
- Any nested Stack navigator for feature-specific modals

#### Modal Types
We support two main modal presentation types that should be used based on the specific UI/UX requirements:

1. **Full Modal (Standard)**: Uses `presentation: "modal"`, slides up from bottom and takes full screen height.
2. **Form Sheet Modal**: Uses `presentation: "formSheet"`, can be configured for different heights and appearances.

The following height options are **only available for Form Sheet modals** (`presentation: "formSheet"`):
- Half-height vs. full-height: Controlled by `sheetAllowedDetents` property
- Initial height: Controlled by `sheetInitialDetentIndex` property
- Grabber visibility: Controlled by `sheetGrabberVisible` property

Always ask product/design team to specify which modal type to use for each feature.

#### Modal Screen Configuration
Configure modal screen options correctly between layout and route files:

```typescript
// ✅ Good: Split configuration between layout and route files

// app/_layout.tsx - Define modal behavior
<Stack>
  <Stack.Screen name="(tabs)" options={{ headerShown: false }} />
  <Stack.Screen name="account" options={{ presentation: "modal", animation: "slide_from_bottom" }} />
  <Stack.Screen name="half-sheet" options={{
    presentation: "formSheet",
    animation: "slide_from_bottom",
    sheetCornerRadius: 30,
    sheetGrabberVisible: true,
    sheetInitialDetentIndex: 0,
    sheetAllowedDetents: [0.5, 1.0], // Allows both half and full height
  }} />
</Stack>

// app/account.tsx - Configure UI-specific options
import { Stack } from "expo-router"
import { ScrollView, YStack, PortalProvider } from "tamagui"

export default function AccountModal() {
  return (
    <PortalProvider>
      <Stack.Screen
        options={{
          headerLeft: () => <CloseButton />,
          headerTitleAlign: "center",
        }}
      />
      <ScrollView contentInsetAdjustmentBehavior="automatic">
        <YStack gap="$4" p="$4">
          {/* Modal content */}
        </YStack>
      </ScrollView>
    </PortalProvider>
  )
}

// ❌ Bad: Mixing behavior and UI options in route file
// app/account.tsx
export default function AccountModal() {
  return (
    <>
      <Stack.Screen
        options={{
          presentation: "modal", // ❌ Should be in layout
          headerLeft: () => <CloseButton />,
          headerTitleAlign: "center",
        }}
      />
      {/* ... */}
    </>
  )
}

// ❌ Bad: Putting UI options in layout file
// app/_layout.tsx
<Stack>
  <Stack.Screen
    name="account"
    options={{
      presentation: "modal",
      headerLeft: () => <CloseButton />, // ❌ Should be in route
      headerTitleAlign: "center", // ❌ Should be in route
    }}
  />
</Stack>
```

Key requirements for modal screens:
- Define modal behavior options (`presentation`, `animation`, etc.) in the layout file
- Configure UI-specific options (header styling, buttons) in the route file
- Can be placed at any level of the navigation hierarchy
- Access using relative paths within the same navigator or absolute paths from elsewhere

#### Layout File Configuration
Keep layout files focused on navigation structure and behavior:

```typescript
// Root layout (app/_layout.tsx)
<Stack>
  <Stack.Screen name="(tabs)" options={{ headerShown: false }} />
  
  {/* Full Modal */}
  <Stack.Screen name="account" options={{ 
    presentation: "modal", 
    animation: "slide_from_bottom" 
  }} />
  
  {/* Half-Sheet Modal */}
  <Stack.Screen name="half-sheet" options={{
    presentation: "formSheet",
    animation: "slide_from_bottom",
    sheetCornerRadius: 30,
    sheetGrabberVisible: true,
    sheetInitialDetentIndex: 0, // 0 = first value in sheetAllowedDetents (0.5)
    sheetAllowedDetents: [0.5, 1.0], // [half-height, full-height]
  }} />
</Stack>

// Tab layout (app/(tabs)/portfolio/_layout.tsx)
<Stack>
  <Stack.Screen name="index" />
  <Stack.Screen name="filter" options={{ presentation: "modal", animation: "slide_from_bottom" }} />
</Stack>
```

This approach:
- Keeps navigation behavior configuration in layout files
- Keeps UI-specific configuration in route files
- Makes the separation of concerns clear
- Follows Expo Router's best practices

### Modal Configuration Types

#### 1. Full Modal (Standard)
The default modal that slides up from the bottom and takes the full screen height.

```tsx
<Stack.Screen name="standard-modal" options={{ 
  presentation: "modal", 
  animation: "slide_from_bottom" 
}} />
```

#### 2. Form Sheet Modal
A modal with configurable height options. These height configurations ONLY work with `presentation: "formSheet"`.

```tsx
<Stack.Screen name="form-sheet" options={{
  presentation: "formSheet",
  animation: "slide_from_bottom",
  sheetCornerRadius: 30,            // Rounded corners
  sheetGrabberVisible: true,        // Show grabber at top
  sheetInitialDetentIndex: 0,       // Start at first detent (half-height)
  sheetAllowedDetents: [0.5, 1.0],  // Allow half and full heights
}} />
```

Height variations (only for `presentation: "formSheet"`):
- Half-height only: `sheetAllowedDetents: [0.5]`
- Full-height only: `sheetAllowedDetents: [1.0]`
- Start at full-height: `sheetInitialDetentIndex: 1`

#### 3. Half-Sheet Modal
A modal that can be displayed at half-height or full-height, with a grabber for user interaction.

```tsx
<Stack.Screen name="half-sheet" options={{
  presentation: "formSheet",
  animation: "slide_from_bottom",
  sheetCornerRadius: 30,            // Rounded corners
  sheetGrabberVisible: true,        // Show grabber at top
  sheetInitialDetentIndex: 0,       // Start at first detent (half-height)
  sheetAllowedDetents: [0.5, 1.0],  // Allow half and full heights
}} />
```

Variations:
- Half-height only: `sheetAllowedDetents: [0.5]`
- Full-height only: `sheetAllowedDetents: [1.0]`
- Start at full-height: `sheetInitialDetentIndex: 1`

### 4. Modal Type Selection
- Consult with product/design on which modal type to use for each feature:
  - **Full Modal** (`presentation: "modal"`): Use for content that requires full screen
  - **Form Sheet Modal** (`presentation: "formSheet"`): Use when content doesn't need full screen, with options for half or full height
- Document the chosen modal type in technical specifications
- Remember that height configuration options (half/full height) are ONLY available for Form Sheet modals

